From: Kevin Shenavai <24247382+krecks@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] devfreq: mtk-cci: leave a disabled OPP, run at the resolved rate

mtk-svs init01 disables every CCI OPP except the fastest one that runs at
vboot and then checks that the buck shared by the LITTLE cluster and the
CCI reads vboot. mtk-cci-devfreq only handled OPP_EVENT_ADJUST_VOLTAGE,
so the CCI stayed on its disabled OPP and kept the buck above vboot:

  SVSB_CPU_LITTLE: buck voltage: 893750uV, expected vboot: 800000uV
  mtk-svs 1100bc00.svs: error -EPERM: svs start fail

Retarget when the current OPP is disabled, as mediatek-cpufreq does.

mtk_ccifreq_target() also looked the voltage up from the nearest
available OPP but set the PLL to the requested rate. With the requested
OPP disabled that runs the CCI faster than its voltage allows. Use the
resolved rate for the clock and for pre_freq.
---
--- a/drivers/devfreq/mtk-cci-devfreq.c	2026-11-08 21:59:59.227581460 +0100
+++ b/drivers/devfreq/mtk-cci-devfreq.c	2026-11-08 21:59:59.243214218 +0100
@@ -134,9 +134,6 @@
 	if (!drv)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	if (drv->pre_freq == *freq)
-		return 0;
-
 	mutex_lock(&drv->reg_lock);
 
 	inter_voltage = drv->inter_voltage;
@@ -153,6 +150,17 @@
 	voltage = dev_pm_opp_get_voltage(opp);
 	dev_pm_opp_put(opp);
 
+	/*
+	 * The requested OPP can be disabled (mtk-svs disables all but one
+	 * while it calibrates). Run at the OPP that was found, so the clock
+	 * never runs faster than the voltage that was looked up allows.
+	 */
+	*freq = opp_rate;
+	if (drv->pre_freq == *freq) {
+		ret = 0;
+		goto out_unlock;
+	}
+
 	pre_voltage = regulator_get_voltage(drv->proc_reg);
 	if (pre_voltage < 0) {
 		dev_err(dev, "invalid vproc value: %d\n", pre_voltage);
@@ -237,6 +245,25 @@
 			mtk_ccifreq_set_voltage(drv, volt);
 		}
 		mutex_unlock(&drv->reg_lock);
+	} else if (event == OPP_EVENT_DISABLE) {
+		bool current_opp;
+
+		mutex_lock(&drv->reg_lock);
+		current_opp = dev_pm_opp_get_freq(opp) == drv->pre_freq;
+		mutex_unlock(&drv->reg_lock);
+
+		/*
+		 * The OPP the CCI runs at was disabled. Pick a new target now,
+		 * like mediatek-cpufreq does for the CPUs: mtk-svs disables
+		 * all but its vboot OPP and then expects the shared buck to be
+		 * at vboot. Staying on the disabled OPP keeps that buck high
+		 * and SVS init fails with "buck voltage ... expected vboot".
+		 */
+		if (current_opp) {
+			mutex_lock(&drv->devfreq->lock);
+			update_devfreq(drv->devfreq);
+			mutex_unlock(&drv->devfreq->lock);
+		}
 	}
 
 	return 0;
